@startuml
' Figure 7.1 – Use case diagram, Sprint 7: comment likes, lists, block, save, share
' FR-60…FR-65 (US-56, US-57, US-58).
!include ../_style.puml
left to right direction
skinparam nodesep 14
skinparam ranksep 60

actor "Visitor" as VIS
actor "Free reader" as LG

LG --|> VIS

rectangle {
  usecase "Sign in" as AUTH
  usecase "Share an article\nor a profile" as SHARE
  usecase "View followers and\nfollowing lists" as LISTS
  usecase "Follow or unfollow\nfrom a list" as FOLLOW
  usecase "Like a comment\nor a reply" as CLIKE
  usecase "Save an article" as SAVE
  usecase "Review saved\narticles" as SAVED
  usecase "Block an account" as BLOCK
  usecase "Review and lift\none's blocks" as UNBLOCK
  usecase "Notify the\nrecipient" as NOTIF
}

VIS --> SHARE
VIS --> LISTS
LG  --> CLIKE
LG  --> SAVE
LG  --> SAVED
LG  --> BLOCK
LG  --> UNBLOCK

LISTS   <.. FOLLOW : <<extend>>
FOLLOW  ..> AUTH : <<include>>
CLIKE   ..> AUTH : <<include>>
CLIKE   ..> NOTIF : <<include>>
SAVE    ..> AUTH : <<include>>
SAVE    ..> NOTIF : <<include>>
SAVED   ..> AUTH : <<include>>
BLOCK   ..> AUTH : <<include>>
UNBLOCK ..> AUTH : <<include>>

VIS -[hidden]down- LG

note as N1
  Sharing and the follower lists need no account:
  a link to X, Facebook or LinkedIn, or a copied URL,
  opens for anyone (FR-61, FR-65).
end note

note as N2
  A block hides both accounts from each other, and the
  only place to lift it is the blocker's settings
  (FR-62). Saves are private: the author is notified,
  but no one else can see what a reader keeps (FR-64).
end note
@enduml
